## Understanding Command-Line Arguments in Dart

The list `args` contains all command-line arguments passed to your program. Each argument is a string and can be accessed by index or iterated over using loops like `for-in`. This makes it easy to parse user input dynamically at runtime.















​```Dart

import 'dart:io';  

List<String> colors = []; // Initialize an empty list to store color names  

// Define the main function with access to command-line arguments  
void main() {  
  List<String> args = Platform  
      .arguments; // Get all command-line arguments from Platform.arguments  

  // Loop through each argument starting from index 1 (skip "--colors")  
  for (int i = 1; i < args.length; i++) {  
    String currentArg = args[i];  

    // Check if the current argument starts with "-"  
    if (currentArg.startsWith('-')) {  
      continue; // Skip any flag-like arguments that start with "-"  
    } else {  
      colors.add(currentArg); // Add valid color name to the list  
    }  
  }  

  // Print out all collected color names  
  print(colors);  

  // Optional debugging output showing what was added  
  for (String color in colors) {  
    print(color); // Prints each individual color on its own line  
  }  
}  

```  




## Example Usage

​```bash    

./app.dart --colors red green blue  
[green, blue]





#### What’s happening here?
- The script reads only non-flag values (`red`, `green`) into the `colors` list.
- Flags like `--colors` are ignored because they begin with `-`.
- You now have a clean list of chosen colors without extra noise.




















